cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "ALEPH-Bench: A Rate–Distortion Benchmark for Prompt Elicitation Efficiency"
message: "If you use ALEPH-Bench, please cite both the software and the paper."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: <FIRST>
    family-names: <LAST>
    affiliation: <ORG>
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"
repository-code: "<REPO_URL>"
url: "<LEADERBOARD_URL>"
abstract: >-
  ALEPH-Bench measures elicitation efficiency: for a fixed target output, the shortest prompt a model
  needs to reproduce it at a required fidelity, reported as a per-model rate–distortion frontier
  (area-under-curve AURC, with interpretable duals ECL@tau and Elicit@k). It evaluates models
  output-first and black-box, with a hard leakage gate, meta-evaluated fidelity metrics, contamination-
  controlled splits, and an information-theoretic white-box track. Scores are honest upper bounds on
  the model-relative description length and predict production prompt cost.
keywords:
  - large language models
  - benchmark
  - prompt compression
  - rate-distortion
  - description length
  - elicitation efficiency
